## 11. CENTRAL CONTENT MANAGEMENT THROUGH STATIC DATA

Create one central article dataset.

Do not hardcode different versions of articles inside individual pages.

Use a strongly typed Article interface.

Recommended TypeScript structure:

```ts
type Category =
  | "health"
  | "finance"
  | "family";

type ArticleBlock =
  | { type: "paragraph"; text: string }
  | { type: "heading"; level: 2 | 3; text: string }
  | { type: "list"; ordered: boolean; items: string[] }
  | { type: "quote"; text: string; attribution?: string }
  | { type: "callout"; title?: string; text: string }
  | { type: "image"; src: string; alt: string; caption?: string };

interface Article {
  id: string;
  slug: string;
  title: string;
  category: Category;
  excerpt: string;
  coverImage: string;
  coverImageAlt: string;
  authorId: string;
  publishedAt: string;
  updatedAt?: string;
  readingTime: number;
  tags: string[];
  blocks: ArticleBlock[];
  relatedArticleIds: string[];
  featured: boolean;
}
```

The actual implementation may extend these interfaces where necessary.

### Data integrity requirements

- Every article must have a unique ID.
- Every article must have a unique slug.
- Every category must be valid.
- Every article must have a valid author reference.
- Every related article reference must point to an existing article.
- Every image must have a valid source or fallback.
- Publication dates must use consistent ISO date formatting.
- Reading times must be realistic.
- All articles must contain meaningful content.

Create at least nine complete sample articles.

Use reusable helper functions for:
- Article lookup by slug.
- Category filtering.
- Search.
- Related article selection.
- Reading-time calculations where needed.

Do not create a database.

Do not make fake API requests to retrieve locally stored content.

